- The publisher node is called chatter and then the subscriber subscribed to this named node (ex2p1.2)
When you create a topic in ros, messages are exchanged between nodes, but the message type must be a single type of message (string, JPEG image, etc)

Seems to be a helpful link to understand how to do wheel odometry with duckiebots specifically: https://docs-old.duckietown.org/daffy/duckietown-robotics-development/out/odometry_modeling.html
-- It shows that distance travelled by the WHEELS each, x
x = 2 * pi * R * (N_ticks / N_total)
N_total for our duckiebot is 135
N_ticks gotten via subscribing to the encoder reader topic
R can be achieved by getting the parameter for kinematics_node/radius topic. Remember that we even set the trim param, and were able to see the radius as one of the params

Note that while the videos were pretty accurate, it took multiple tries, there were always deviations and it usually has to do with slippage, wheel wobble, callibration, ground material etc. We assume perfect rolling in our calculations, but it's not always the case. Another case might be that the comparison that distance has been reached or angle has been rotated might be happening when the current distance/angle has already overshooted the target distance/angle by a considerable amount

For the rotation task
Rotating on the spot, we know that arc_length = d * theta where d is the distance to centre of the bot. we also know that arc length for each wheel is 2 * pi * R (N_ticks/N_total_ticks)

Therefore, we can calculte theta as (arc_length_left - arc_length_right)/d

Note that we KNOW the arc_length is the same as x above simply because x is the distance travelled by the wheels and while rotating on the spot, the bot may have not travelled any distance, the wheels have still travelled a distance equal to the arc length.

Measured 0.1 as d

I tried being cheeky and added a rospy.sleep() but that messed everything up

For Part II.5: Our programs did, in fact exit properly after we finish respective tasks. Potential reasons for why it may not shutdown is that the ros nodes could still be active after our particular task is ended, especially with ros commands like spin() which keep subscriber nodes alive even after it reaches the end of the python script to keep listening to messages and that the ros shutdown() command isn't called. 
In our code, we don't have a call to rospy.spin(), we also have a break statement in our while loop whose condition is to check if rospy.is_shutdown() is true or not. After exiting the while loop, in our main function, we call rospy.signal_shutdown() as well, ensuring that the ROS master terminates all nodes.

